 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Charge current from the ISET voltage",
   "expr": "IBAT = (VISET / 1.0V) × ICHG",
   "where": {
    "IBAT": "present charge current",
    "ICHG": "programmed CC-mode current",
    "VISET": "voltage on ISET at any point in the cycle"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 11
  },
  {
   "name": "CC-mode current",
   "expr": "ICHG = 4000 / (RISET + 96)",
   "where": {
    "ICHG": "CC-mode charge current, A",
    "RISET": "resistor from ISET to ground, Ω"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 14
  },
  {
   "name": "Current limit at low headroom",
   "expr": "IBAT ≤ (VIN - VBAT) / RDS(ON)",
   "where": {
    "RDS(ON)": "on resistance of the internal power MOSFET"
   },
   "note": "When this is below ICHG, it sets the charge current.",
   "page": 15
  },
  {
   "name": "Minimum input for full CC current",
   "expr": "VIN > VBAT + ICHG × 475mΩ",
   "where": {
    "475mΩ": "worst-case RDS(ON)",
    "ICHG": "programmed CC-mode current"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 17
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Charge profile",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Three phases: trickle, constant current (CC), then constant voltage (CV); CC and CV together count as fast charge. Once VIN passes the power-on-reset level, PPR goes low and charging starts in trickle mode with CHG low. Trickle current is 20% of ICHG and continues until the cell exceeds 2.7 V; a cell that never rises stays in trickle. At 2.7 V the part ramps gently into CC, which limits the dip on the input and eases the input capacitor requirement; FAST goes low. At 4.2 V it holds the output at 4.2 V and the current tapers."
  },
  {
   "topic": "End of charge and recharge",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "When the tapering current falls to IEOC (10% of ICHG), CHG goes high-impedance. The output is still held at 4.2 V, so a load beside the cell keeps being fed; a load above ICHG draws the rest from the cell. When the cell falls below the recharge threshold (4.1 V typ) the part returns to fast charge and CHG goes low again."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown conditions",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The charger turns off if VIN exceeds VOVP, if VIN - VBAT drops below VOS, or if EN is high. VOS is the offset of the comparator that watches VIN against BAT; it stops the cell leaking back into an unpowered input. Disabled or unpowered, BAT draws under 1.0 µA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input power-good range",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Charging needs all three: VIN > VPOR, VIN - VBAT > VOS and VIN < VOVP. The VIN pin tolerates 28 V, so no separate input over-voltage circuit is required; above the 6.8 V OVP threshold the charger is simply held off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Charge current setting and monitoring",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "RISET from ISET to ground sets ICHG = 4000 / (RISET + 96). The ISET current is proportional to the charge current, so the ISET voltage (1.0 V at full CC current) gives the actual current throughout the cycle. A 1% resistor is called for to hold the 6% current accuracy."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Charge current limits",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "With little headroom, (VIN - VBAT) / RDS(ON) may be less than ICHG and then sets the current. With a large VIN - VBAT difference, dissipation can heat the die into thermal foldback."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal foldback",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "An internal loop starts cutting the charge current when the die reaches 110 °C (TLIMIT), so the die heats no further. ICHG can therefore be chosen for the typical ambient rather than the worst case; the part trims the current itself under worst-case heat."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Status outputs",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "PPR depends only on VIN: low above VPOR, unaffected by EN, OVP or the VIN-BAT comparator, and sinks at least 12 mA. CHG is low while enabled and charging, high-impedance once complete, when disabled or when VIN is out of range; it sinks at least 12 mA. FAST is low in fast charge (battery above the trickle threshold) and high-impedance in trickle, when disabled or out of range; it needs a pull-up."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Enable",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "EN has a weak internal pull-down. Low, open or grounded, it enables the charger while VIN is in range; high, it disables it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "State sequence",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Power off (VIN < VPOR) leads to shutdown once VIN > VPOR. From shutdown, EN low with VIN < VOVP and VIN > VBAT + VOS starts trickle charge. Trickle moves to fast charge when VBAT > 2.7 V and back when VBAT < 2.7 V. Fast charge ends in charge complete when VBAT = 4.2 V and IBAT < IEOC; charge complete returns to fast charge when VBAT < 4.10 V. EN high, VIN > VOVP or VIN < VBAT + VOS from any charging state returns to shutdown, and VIN < VPOR to power off."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input capacitor",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "A 1.0 µF X5R ceramic rated 16 V suits most designs; it damps input transients that could otherwise upset stability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output capacitor",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Use X5R ceramic, at least 2.2 µF nominal, for stability; heavier load transients may call for more."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal layout",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "The 2 × 3 mm UDFN needs care with heat. Solder the exposed pad well to a large ground copper area on the component side; where that side has no room, join the pad to inner ground layers with an array of vias. This lets the part charge at full current with a lower die temperature."
  }
 ],
